inated and then we would  be much better off including replacing  police altogether with social service  agencies or community  watch groups or whatever the case may be  the problem is sometimes too much police  funding for the wrong things the problem  is sometimes lack of training the  problem is not enough funding in some  cases and that can actually be the cause  of the problems we're trying to solve so  let's just get really specific about it  let's start with one example many police  departments have become militarized like  armies this is something I talked about  last week and I mentioned last week and  this is super important police training  in the United States is too short and  doesn't deal with how police officers  should actually be trained to be better  stewards of their communities and to  de-escalate situations rather than  escalate situations in many countries  police training is much longer than in  the United States that costs more money  but that extra training should not be  focused on riot control with rubber  bullets it should instead be social work  type training if police were trained  more like social workers and less like  soldiers if police were equipped with  de-escalation tools rather than military  vehicles then we would eliminate many of  the disastrous outcomes that we have  because the first instinct of more  police would be to de-escalate rather  than to say is this a time to pull a gun  or to use a taser taser or to pull out a  nightstick or baton or shield or police  a military a military vehicle but the  reality is that could cost more money it  could cost more money to better trained  police for longer if you double the  amount of time police officers spend in  training from 13 to 26 weeks it's likely  that is going to cost more money not  less and so the sledgehammer of abolish  the police or defund the police fails to  actually achieve what we want I  mentioned militarization when a police  department is militarized they see  themselves more like a military force  than as members of the community and  they solve problems with the tools that  they have again shields military type  vehicles riot control tactics  pepper pepper spray balls and the like  in this case we need to demilitarize the  police now you might do that by  strategically cutting funding for  certain types of training and equipment  in order to achieve the demilitarization  of police beautiful brilliant I'm all  for it but again defund the police or  abolish the police does not actually  leave us better off now in some police  departments the problem is they don't  have enough funding they can't hire  enough officers they can't do community  activism and involvement so when a  police department is underfunded they  resort to the cheap policing of what we  call low-hanging fruit if you are  underfunded and police aren't properly  trained and you don't have the resources  you need to get police involved in the  community  you basically just sent up tons of cops  to poor areas you send the cops you have  to poor areas and as we know those areas  are disproportionately non-white and  they hang out there maybe they do stop  and frisk or similar type tactics and  then they just rack up arrest simply by  saying you know we we don't we send  people to the areas where we believe  there is the most crime and of course  because that's the only area they are in  they find crime there and nowhere else  and it gets up there arrests numbers and  it's the self-fulfilling prophecy that  we know so so well so that's an example  where the police department might  actually be better if they were better  funded not to get shoulder mounted RPGs  and riot gear but to actually have  proper levels of staffing to be involved  in the community to be trained in  de-escalation like social workers let me  give you one more example of this a lot  of police are policing places where they  don't live and in fact sometimes there's  a big divide where you